    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d143">

                <pb facs="0291" n="181" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.60p2/19: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Latvia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Coleman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Riga</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-02-21T16:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 21,
                                1927—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received February 21—2:18
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>16. Your instruction number 106 [<hi rendition="#italic">406</hi>], January 21st.
                    Has the Department any objection to combining suitably in article 16 the last
                    paragraph of article 7 beginning “the stipulation[s]” and first eleven lines in
                    last paragraph of article 7 in German treaty?<anchor n="24" xml:id="d143-f1"/>
                    The latter is uniform in all treaties made by Latvia, being now in force between
                    Latvia and Esthonia. Would appreciate reply by cable end of week.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Coleman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
